<?php

/**
 * @file
 * Custom Webform Handlers module.
 */

use Drupal\custom_webform_handlers\Dispatch\Notify\DispatchNotificationDispatcher;

/**
 * Implements hook_mail().
 *
 * Assembly only. The subject and body arrive already composed, from
 * DispatchNotificationComposer, which is where the placeholder vocabulary and the
 * privacy rules that govern it live; this hook decides nothing about content and adds
 * nothing to it.
 *
 * Plain text, declared as such. There is no HTML alternative and no markup here, so
 * there is no escaping decision to get wrong — a customer's own answer can never be
 * rendered into a message as though it were part of the template, because the template
 * is never treated as markup in the first place.
 */
function custom_webform_handlers_mail($key, &$message, $params) {
  if ($key !== DispatchNotificationDispatcher::MAIL_KEY) {
    return;
  }

  // Set rather than appended: this is the module's own key, and inventing a merge with
  // whatever the mail system defaulted to would make the encoding depend on the site's
  // other modules.
  $message['headers']['Content-Type'] = 'text/plain; charset=utf-8';
  $message['headers']['Content-Transfer-Encoding'] = '8Bit';

  $message['subject'] = (string) ($params['subject'] ?? '');

  // The body is an array of lines that the mail system joins. One element, because the
  // composed body is already the whole message including its line breaks.
  $message['body'][] = (string) ($params['body'] ?? '');
}

/**
 * Implements hook_cron().
 *
 * The module's unattended hands, doing two unrelated jobs that share only the fact that
 * no member of staff is present to do them. Each runs in its own try/catch with no path
 * between them, because one is an authority the site depends on and the other is a
 * convenience it does not, and a queue that is down for the second must never be able to
 * stop the first.
 *
 * The first job is the Review Request expiry sweep, and it exists because a deadline has
 * to be able to pass without anyone watching it. Every other transition on a request is
 * somebody's decision and belongs to the form that records who made it. Expiry is the
 * exception — nobody expires a quote, a date does — which is why
 * ReviewManagerInterface::expireLapsed() takes no identifier and cannot be aimed at a
 * particular customer's job from here. It is bounded at the manager's own default: a
 * site offline long enough to accumulate more lapsed offers than one run will sweep
 * clears the rest on the following run, a slower cleanup than a single unbounded UPDATE
 * and a far smaller thing to go wrong, because cron holds no lock over the whole table
 * and a customer whose approved offer is being paid for right now is racing a
 * compare-and-set inside the manager rather than a sweep that already claimed every row
 * it read. The state machine, not this hook, decides what may lapse —
 * ReviewState::mayExpire() is FALSE for a scheduled booking, so a job somebody has
 * already paid for cannot be expired out from under them no matter how long ago its
 * offer deadline was set.
 *
 * The second job only asks for a recount of the Service Type popularity snapshot; it
 * never computes one. That snapshot is a read-cache that orders the service list, not an
 * authority — the ranking has a deterministic fallback and Express Courier is pinned
 * first regardless — so the work is a non-authoritative Advanced Queue job that runs on
 * its own SQL-backed worker, and cron's whole part in it is to enqueue, never to hold a
 * maintenance run open behind a recount. It is throttled to roughly nightly by a single
 * state timestamp so an hourly cron does not enqueue an hourly recount, and it is the
 * dependable driver rather than the only one: an ECA reaction enqueues the same job
 * opportunistically when a Review Request transitions, and this hook is what guarantees
 * the snapshot is still refreshed on a quiet day when nothing transitioned. The job
 * forbids duplicates and the recount is idempotent, so the two drivers overlapping costs
 * nothing.
 *
 * Both failures are caught and named by class rather than by message, the same
 * discipline the rest of the module follows for storage and provider errors, because an
 * exception message can quote the values it choked on and cron writes to a log that
 * operators read. Cron continues either way: the other modules on the site have their
 * own maintenance to do and none of it depends on either of these.
 */
function custom_webform_handlers_cron(): void {
  try {
    \Drupal::service('custom_webform_handlers.review_manager')->expireLapsed();
  }
  catch (\Throwable $exception) {
    // Logged, not rethrown. The count of what did lapse is the manager's own to
    // report; what is recorded here is only that the sweep stopped early, so a run
    // that quietly did nothing cannot be mistaken for a run that found nothing.
    \Drupal::logger('custom_webform_handlers')->error('The Review Request expiry sweep failed: @class.', [
      '@class' => get_class($exception),
    ]);
  }

  try {
    // Roughly nightly, not every run. Cron may fire many times a day; a read-cache does
    // not earn a recount that often, so a single state timestamp gates it to once per
    // window. The window is stamped before the enqueue, not after, so a process that
    // dies between the two cannot turn one missed recount into a recount every minute —
    // the snapshot simply waits for the next window, which for a non-authoritative cache
    // is the safe direction to fail. The enqueue itself swallows its own errors, so the
    // catch below is for the unlikely failure of reaching the queue service at all.
    $state = \Drupal::state();
    $now = \Drupal::time()->getRequestTime();
    $last = (int) $state->get('custom_webform_handlers.popularity_recount.last_enqueued', 0);
    // Twenty hours: comfortably inside a day, so a nightly cron never drifts far enough
    // to skip a night, and comfortably outside an hour, so an hourly cron never enqueues
    // hourly.
    if ($now - $last >= 72000) {
      $state->set('custom_webform_handlers.popularity_recount.last_enqueued', $now);
      \Drupal::service('custom_webform_handlers.maintenance_queue')->enqueuePopularityRecount();
    }
  }
  catch (\Throwable $exception) {
    // Independent of the sweep above and logged the same way. A recount that could not
    // be enqueued leaves the previous snapshot in place, and the ranking's deterministic
    // fallback behind that; nothing a customer sees depends on this having run.
    \Drupal::logger('custom_webform_handlers')->error('The Service Type popularity recount could not be enqueued: @class.', [
      '@class' => get_class($exception),
    ]);
  }
}
